-## Account allowance
-
-The existing connection owner reads allowance for a requested saved account, or its configured
-active account when the account ID is absent, using the existing host network transport and OAuth
-refresh. Reading an inactive account never selects it, changes failure/recovery health, redeems
-credits or starts inference. Quota inspection bypasses inference failover selection. Both paths
-share the same credential-refresh function under the existing connection lock; no new refresh or
-credential authority is introduced. Cindy/cc-switch's Codex WHAM reader and cc-switch's Copilot
-user reader and Grok Build's `extensions/billing.rs` GET `/v1/billing?format=credits` are acquisition references; these vendor-private endpoints can change. Orca's native
-Codex RPC is not present in Pi and does not justify adding another Agent runtime. Cockpit Tools
-is comparison evidence only. Cache entries are independent per connection/account and keyed by
-connection revision, provider kind and credential fingerprint, coalesce concurrent reads, and expire
-after 60 seconds; a forced read bypasses freshness but joins a pending request. Transient allowance
-or credential-refresh failure retains dated last-good data; allowance authentication failure clears
-it. An opaque OAuth-driver exception is unavailable, not proof that authentication was revoked. Results carry the
-internal account ID and connection revision; the optional fields preserve parser/legacy compatibility.
-Manual refresh is available; requests are redirect-free and account-bound;
-they never enable paid overage or change account/model selection. Account/revision changes while
-fetching discard the response, including deletion, selection and reauthorization. Authentication,
-rate limits, unsupported and unavailable stay distinct. No account failure rotates a quota read to
-another account. The sequence is: capture requested identity → account-scoped freshness/coalescing →
-refresh credentials under the existing lock when a read is needed → acquire allowance → revalidate
-identity/revision/token → publish the dated result.
-The settings account section reuses ZCode's status-card and Progress primitives. It shows valid
-native windows, remaining percentage, reset and observation time; missing values never become 0% or
-unlimited. Subscription allowance does not feed the Session context ring. No foreign app login
-files are read. A pinned adapter model catalog remains labelled as such, not account entitlement.
-
-Codex membership metadata uses the same saved OAuth account and network policy. Read the exact
-workspace entitlement from account-check, then query `/subscriptions?account_id=…` when needed;
-never select an unrelated default/first paid workspace. Both calls share an eight-second deadline.
-The explicit account parameter and Cockpit's browser/route headers are required by the verified live path; these two metadata calls omit ChatGPT-Account-Id and bind identity by query/response instead. OAuth, quota and inference headers stay unchanged; no browser cookies are imported. Membership
-metadata caches for fifteen minutes inside the same account/revision-scoped usage owner; explicit
-refresh also rereads it. Validity and renewal are separate: `expires_at` is an entitlement term,
-`active_until` plus `will_renew` describes the current billing term. Preserve dated last-good metadata
-on failure. Do not infer Pro multipliers or dates from token expiry, quota resets or another app's
-files. This is an independent adapter informed by Cockpit's acquisition behavior, not imported code.
-
-Kimi Code membership uses its official third-party API-key endpoint, not a copied official-client OAuth identity.
-
-The provider picker presents one entry per explicitly declared vendor, with search and a custom
-provider action. Cindy supplies vendor-first selection; the owner's follow-up requires no dialog
-or dropdown for choosing a connection. NewMax's installed model setup keeps selection and the
-connection form in one content slot. Fleet therefore replaces the gallery in place with a local
-setup draft: visible service/plan choices, regional choices only when multiple regions exist,
-then address and credentials. Declared formats use directly visible segments and save immediately
-through the existing Provider owner. Custom endpoints expose the supported segmented formats
-directly. There is no protocol probing or failure-driven protocol switch. The visual
-values/primitives remain ZCode's. Subscription choices show account connection instead of API fields.
-Selecting a vendor or switching a route never writes a Provider. Back discards only the local
-setup draft. Existing providers and their credentials are not changed. An explicit Add persists
-API credentials through the existing initialConfig facade; explicit Connect continues through
-the existing subscription control. No automatic model inference or paid validation is introduced.
-
-Optional template catalog metadata declares vendor identity/name, service identity/name and
-region. The existing schema, template and facade carry it; grouping is only a UI projection.
-Legacy category remains readable but no longer determines presentation. Templates without
-metadata remain independent. Search matches vendor and variant names in both locales.
-Service/region changes select only declared routes and keep drafts isolated by template ID, so
-keys do not silently cross regions or API/subscription boundaries. Protocol switching among
-explicit route variants selects the actual matching template; custom API configuration remains
-available. Creation has an immediate local lock, preserves the draft/error on failure and retries
