-## Multiple credentials — implementation contract
-
-The owner confirmed automatic switching only within the same service and exact model. Keep the
-address/protocol first, with multiple key or subscription-account rows below. Following NewMax's
-account-rotation panel, a separate full-width surface below the Add action holds the title and
-eligibility hint on the left and the opt-in switch on the right (default off, requires two distinct
-nonempty keys or connected accounts). Empty/duplicate key rows do not enable it. Connection and
-model-list sections have distinct React identities, including after changing provider or refreshing.
-NewMax 1.1.18 supplies the account-list and
-failure-recovery comparison; its separate model-priority fallback is not part of this request.
-
-Extend the existing Provider facade, SubscriptionConnection owner, CredentialService and runtime
-request-auth seam. API access retains its existing primary key and adds optional backup keys and
-an opt-in flag in the same provider config. Subscription records add accounts under the same
-encrypted credential entry; legacy single credentials are read as one account without a bulk
-migration. Account selection/removal/login mutations use the existing connection file lock and
-invalidate pending flows. UI receives account metadata, never OAuth refresh/access tokens.
-
-Every Codex/Grok model-bound credential resolution verifies that exact model in the resolved
-account's native catalog before releasing request credentials, including the configured default
-and recovery accounts. Configured rows and Pi's static list are not account entitlement evidence.
-A private derived catalog cache in this owner is keyed by connection, provider kind, internal
-account, connection revision, pending-flow identity and credential fingerprint, with a 60-second
-TTL and coalesced reads. Selection, removal and reauthorization invalidate pending/cached entries;
-publication rechecks the saved identity. Catalog failure or a missing model fails closed, without
-trying another account/provider or returning an expired success. Explicit model discovery reads
-and refreshes the configured active account under the existing credential lock, never inference
-failover selection, then refreshes the shared catalog cache without changing failure health.
-The refresh lock also preserves a successfully rotated credential before honoring local request
-cancellation: queued account mutations still win after the lock is released, but cancellation
-cannot discard the only valid rotated refresh token. A persisted identity/revision change prevents
-saving stale refresh material. No new credential store, schema or public service is introduced.
-Every admitted attempt must retain provider, endpoint, protocol and model; only its eligible
-credential can change. Distinguish authentication failure from model entitlement, rate limiting,
-policy rejection and transport uncertainty. Never turn a generic 403 into unconditional rotation.
-The transport may change credentials only after a rejected HTTP 401 or account-specific 429,
-before returning a success stream. Each identity is visited once, with at most ten attempts and
-a two-minute admission deadline; cancellation closes the current attempt. 403/policy errors,
-shared organization/project rate limits, network uncertainty and streams never rotate. Exhaustion
-is terminal for outer SDK/workflow retries, including an unbounded workflow budget.
-
-Failure metadata lives with its existing owner: API transport health is scoped to the model
-execution instance (hashed credential identifiers, no second secret store; reset at process restart); subscription health is
-saved under each account in the existing encrypted connection entry, under its existing lock.
-401/policy failures require explicit reconnect/reconfiguration. Quota failures respect provider
-reset times, or remain unavailable until explicit account repair if no reset is known. Temporary
-throttling respects Retry-After and uses bounded exponential cooldown if absent. Shared limits
-block the pool; they are not bypassed by changing keys. Only one expired API cooldown probe is
-admitted at once; no timer sends background model requests. A late success cannot erase a newer
-failure. Explicit account selection can retry a repaired account; ordinary page changes cannot.
-Unknown transport outcomes are not replayed. Health isolation never changes endpoint, protocol,
-model, subscription/API billing mode or the owner's selected primary account.
-
-```mermaid
-sequenceDiagram
-  participant Run as Existing model request
-  participant Owner as Existing credential / transport owner
-  participant Vendor as Selected provider
-  Run->>Owner: Select eligible identity, retain exact model and route
-  Owner->>Vendor: One attempt
-  Vendor-->>Owner: Rejected request + reset / failure facts
-  Owner->>Owner: Record failure, exclude visited / unavailable identities
-  alt Eligible backup within total budget
-    Owner->>Vendor: One attempt using backup
-  else Exhausted, shared limit, policy rejection or uncertainty
-    Owner-->>Run: Terminal failure, no outer retry loop
-  end
-```
-
-Research anchors: CC Switch `proxy/circuit_breaker.rs` single half-open permit; OpenClaw
-`auth-profiles/usage-failure-state.ts` separates cooldown from disabled state; Cockpit Tools
-`codex_local_access_quota_cooldown.rs` retains known exhausted readings when refresh fails.
-OpenAI's rate-limit guide requires bounded attempts/time and accounting for SDK retries; account
-rotation is not proof of permission or protection from suspension. Preserve subscription/provider
-restrictions, no identity disguising or circumvention of a shared limit.
-
-NewMax's Antigravity path has its own OAuth/account/catalog and request-conversion adapter. Its
-presence is not evidence of supported third-party consumer access: Google's current Antigravity
-terms explicitly restrict that OAuth usage. The documented API-key/enterprise SDK is a distinct
-Agent harness. Neither an Antigravity subscription preset nor an additional executor is added here.
