-## In-turn subscription affinity (OV-044)
-
-The active AgentRuntime turn owns its resolved saved-account reference, per provider, until the
-turn ends or a new user Guide is admitted at the existing model-step boundary. The existing subscription owner still owns all credentials, health and refresh. A
-settings default change affects the next turn, not later model/tool steps of an admitted turn.
-The first successful auth resolution binds the account; same-account refresh remains allowed.
-A removed account fails before another model request rather than silently following a new default.
-Explicitly enabled bounded rejection recovery remains the only exception and updates that turn's
-binding after success. Ending/stopping the active turn discards the derived references. A late auth
-reply cannot bind or execute in a different active turn or a newly admitted Guide. API keys retain their existing physical
-request enablement checks. No new Host business-state cache, credential store or Session authority.
-Signed/encrypted reasoning carries the generating connection, model and saved-account reference in
-its existing provider metadata. Same-scope replay preserves it; changed scope keeps visible text and
-tool call/result links without reusing opaque state. Rejected-request recovery rebuilds portable
-input before changing account; incomplete server-only continuations stop. Cache keys are account-scoped.
-
-Active turn → resolve through subscription owner → capture account reference → later model calls
-            → same account / exact-model catalog validation → turn ends and reference expires.
-
-## Fast mode (OV-044)
-
-Fast is a per-submission option, separate from effort. The existing model option spec declares an
-optional wire mapping: OpenAI priority tier or Anthropic fast speed. Missing support is unknown;
-null is explicitly unavailable. Selection defaults to standard, and choosing another model does
-not carry a paid fast preference implicitly. Catalog metadata and verified official route/model
-facts may declare support; a model name or OpenAI-compatible URL alone cannot.
-
-The existing ModelOptionMap request owner applies the speed field and required protocol header.
-Standard explicitly requests OpenAI's default tier; Anthropic standard omits its opt-in speed/header.
-Native Codex uses its advertised service tiers and the same frozen choice. Account recovery must
-also support the requested mode; it cannot silently substitute lower effort or standard service.
-The owner rejected the standalone Fast control. The existing effort menu now has separate reasoning
-and speed radio groups; the selected model label contains no provider prefix. The speed group shows
-the sourced allowance/rate increase. It never automatically enables fast mode. Verification uses injected transports, not
-live premium requests. Actual served tier and requested tier are distinct when the provider reports
-a downgrade; do not label a request accelerated merely because the switch was on.
